/// Client cookie storage, the equivalent of Paper's 1.20.5+ ClientCookie API.
///
/// Cookies are small blobs (at most 5120 bytes, the vanilla 5 KiB limit) the
/// server stores on a Java Edition client; the client persists them across
/// server transfers and hands them back when the server asks. Typical uses
/// are session tokens and per-player preferences that must survive a proxy
/// transfer.
///
/// Every function takes the player whose client is addressed. Cookies are a
/// Java Edition protocol feature: for Bedrock players `store-cookie`,
/// `request-cookie` and `clear-cookie` return an error and `get-cookie`
/// returns `none`.
///
/// `store-cookie` and `request-cookie` send play-phase clientbound packets,
/// so they only reach a fully connected (in-game) player. `get-cookie` never
/// sends a packet: it reads the server-side cache of cookie responses the
/// client has sent since connecting (responses can arrive during the login
/// and configuration phases too and are carried over). A key therefore only
/// appears in `get-cookie` once a `request-cookie` round trip has completed.
interface cookie {
    use player.{player};

    /// Stores a cookie on the player's client.
    ///
    /// `key` must be a valid resource identifier: `namespace:path`, or a
    /// plain path (which vanilla treats as the `minecraft` namespace), using
    /// only lowercase letters, digits and `_ . -` (the path may also use
    /// `/`). `payload` must not exceed 5120 bytes; the call fails instead of
    /// truncating.
    ///
    /// # Errors
    /// Returns an error when the key is not a valid identifier, the payload
    /// exceeds the vanilla limit, or the player is a Bedrock client.
    store-cookie: func(player: borrow<player>, key: string, payload: list<u8>) -> result<_, string>;

    /// Asks the player's client for the cookie stored under `key`.
    ///
    /// The request is asynchronous on the wire: when the client's response
    /// arrives it is placed in the server-side cache, readable with
    /// `get-cookie`.
    ///
    /// # Errors
    /// Returns an error when the key is not a valid identifier or the player
    /// is a Bedrock client.
    request-cookie: func(player: borrow<player>, key: string) -> result<_, string>;

    /// Returns the cached value of the cookie `key` for this player, or
    /// `none` when the client has not reported one (yet). Never sends a
    /// packet. Bedrock players always yield `none`.
    get-cookie: func(player: borrow<player>, key: string) -> option<list<u8>>;

    /// Clears the cookie `key`: drops it from the server-side cache and
    /// overwrites the client's copy with an empty payload (vanilla has no
    /// dedicated delete packet, so an empty store is the clear signal).
    ///
    /// # Errors
    /// Returns an error when the key is not a valid identifier or the player
    /// is a Bedrock client.
    clear-cookie: func(player: borrow<player>, key: string) -> result<_, string>;
}
